# .punused-ignore - adjudicated punused findings (EU1001 test-only / EU1002 unused)
#
# The CI gate scans PER DIRECTORY and then requires every reported symbol to be
# deleted, wired up, or matched by a literal substring below. Each non-comment
# line is one such substring, matched against the whole finding line; the " is"
# suffix anchors it to the end of the symbol name so a future symbol with a
# longer name cannot be silently swallowed by an entry here.
#
# What punused (bep/punused v0.5.0) actually does: for every non-_test.go file
# matching its glob it asks gopls for textDocument/documentSymbol, then
# textDocument/references per EXPORTED symbol name, and reports the symbol when
# the reference set is empty (EU1002) or contains only _test.go files (EU1001).
# References are syntactic and workspace-scoped, and its own README lists the
# consequences as caveats: it does not see references from outside the project,
# nor via reflect, and has "some possible surprises when it comes to interfaces".
#
# So three real mechanisms are structurally invisible to it, and every entry
# below is one of them:
#   (a) a method the STANDARD LIBRARY calls through an interface the app never
#       names: encoding/json's json.Unmarshaler, encoding/xml's xml.Unmarshaler,
#       and errors.Is/As/Unwrap's anonymous interface{ Unwrap() error }.
#   (b) a method a DEPENDENCY MODULE calls through its own interface; that
#       module's packages are outside the workspace gopls indexes.
#   (c) an exported constructor whose only callers are _test.go files in OTHER
#       packages, and which no non-test caller can replace.
# (Two further mechanics: the whole punused run shares ONE 30s context, which is
# why the per-directory invocation above is the only one that does not truncate
# silently; and a method on an UNEXPORTED type is still reported when the method
# NAME is exported, which is what most of this file is.)
#
# Verified REAL dead code in this pass was DELETED, not listed here:
# StaleMapError.ConsecutiveRejections (the scout escalates on the persisted
# Cache.RejectedRefreshes and LogAttrs already publishes the streak as
# stale_consecutive_rejections, so the accessor had no production reader) and
# tracker.IsNyaaHost (symmetry-only export with no production caller - its
# AnimeBytes twin has one in filter's AB evidence gate, while a host->tracker
# question reads LookupByHost - so the host-resolution fuzz invariants now run
# against LookupByHost directly).
#
# --- (a) json.Unmarshaler ---------------------------------------------------
# encoding/json type-asserts json.Unmarshaler and calls these; there is no call
# site to find, only a DECODE site whose target embeds the type.
# internal/mapping/fribb.go: fribbRecord's fields (Type flexString, IMDbID
# stringList, TmdbID tmdbID, Season seasonObject) are decoded by
# decodeFribbRecord's json.Unmarshal(msg, &fr) on the production refresh path
# (parseFribbForRefresh -> decodeFribbRecords -> decodeNextFribbRecord).
# internal/anilist/anilist.go: gqlPage.Media (boundedMediaList) is decoded by
# parseMediaPage's json.Unmarshal(raw, &r) on the FetchMany batch path, and
# gqlErrors by envelopeErrors' json.Unmarshal plus the errors[] field of the
# Fetch and batch envelopes. Each of these methods IS the tolerance or the
# cardinality bound on an untrusted upstream body, so deleting one is a
# parsing/resource-exhaustion regression.
method (*seasonObject).UnmarshalJSON is
method (*flexString).UnmarshalJSON is
method (*tmdbID).UnmarshalJSON is
method (*stringList).UnmarshalJSON is
method (*boundedMediaList).UnmarshalJSON is
method (*gqlErrors).UnmarshalJSON is
#
# --- (a) xml.Unmarshaler ----------------------------------------------------
# Same shape, encoding/xml side, all in internal/indexer/torznab.go:
# parseTorznab's xml.Unmarshal decodes feedXML.Channel (channelXML), whose
# decodeChild runs DecodeElement on an itemXML; parseErrorDocument decodes
# errorXML. All three carry the shared *xmlx.Budget decode-time bound, and
# errorXML's method is also what refuses a non-<error> root.
method (*channelXML).UnmarshalXML is
method (*itemXML).UnmarshalXML is
method (*errorXML).UnmarshalXML is
#
# --- (b) httpx.Transient (cross-module interface dispatch) ------------------
# httpx/v4 declares `type Transient interface { IsTransient() bool }` and
# reaches an implementation with errors.As; the retry loop calls it on every
# attempt error. Both types are returned from INSIDE an httpx.Do body, so this
# method IS the retry decision for a 500/408 AniList fault and for a Prowlarr
# timeout or malformed body. A dependency module's interface can never produce
# a workspace reference.
method (*transientStatusError).IsTransient is
method (*transientUpstreamError).IsTransient is
#
# --- (a) errors.Is/As/Unwrap, with a load-bearing non-test consumer ---------
# The stdlib walks a chain through an anonymous interface{ Unwrap() error }, so
# no syntactic reference to Unwrap can exist anywhere. These three are reached
# by a production classification that would change its answer without them:
#  - *BatchRecordError: match's prefetchPass classifies FetchMany's error with
#    errors.Is(err, context.Canceled), and a batch cancelled AFTER its first
#    chunk completed returns exactly this wrapper - without Unwrap a shutdown
#    is misread as a fault.
#  - *walkSideError: scout hands the walk error to httpx.LogSafeError, which
#    does errors.As(&urlErr) to reduce a credential-bearing *url.Error; without
#    Unwrap the reduction stops finding it and a configured userinfo credential
#    can reach the log.
#  - *redactedError: main.go returns it from the report dispatch and render.go
#    wraps report failures in it, while cycle.NormalizeShutdownError and
#    dispatchOutcome classify that same error with errors.Is - the redaction
#    sits UPSTREAM of the classification boundary.
method (*BatchRecordError).Unwrap is
method (*walkSideError).Unwrap is
method (*redactedError).Unwrap is
#
# --- (a) errors.Unwrap, traversed but not excavated ------------------------
# Same invisible mechanism, weaker case, stated plainly: no non-test site reads
# the wrapped cause of these four. They ARE traversed at runtime - httpx's
# classifier probes errors.As(&AuthError), errors.As(&RateLimitError) and
# errors.Is(context.Canceled/DeadlineExceeded) over the whole chain before it
# checks Transient - and they are kept because a wrapper that hides its cause
# is a latent correctness bug, not because someone unwraps them today:
#  - *transientStatusError (internal/anilist/anilist.go) wraps
#    httpx.CheckHTTPStatus's *httpx.HTTPStatusError.
#  - *transientUpstreamError (internal/indexer/prowlarr.go, built by
#    (*upstream).attemptError and classifyParseError) wraps that same status
#    error, or a *torznabLimitError for a decode-limit breach.
#  - *torznabLimitError (internal/indexer/torznab.go) wraps xmlx's *LimitError
#    so errors.Is(err, xmlx.ErrLimit) holds; nothing asks that today (both
#    consumers use errors.AsType[*torznabLimitError], which matches the type
#    itself and needs no unwrap), and the tests assert the library's Kind
#    through the wrap.
#  - *StaleMapError (internal/mapping/mapping.go, built by staleOrFail) wraps
#    the refresh failure it degraded on (a fetch/parse error, an
#    *httpx.ResponseTooLargeError, a sentinel such as errRecordCapExceeded);
#    every production consumer - scout's mappingDegradedAttrs and mapUsable,
#    mapping's own rejectRefresh - matches the TYPE via errors.AsType, so only
#    the mapping tests unwrap it, which is why this one reports EU1001.
method (*transientStatusError).Unwrap is
method (*transientUpstreamError).Unwrap is
method (*torznabLimitError).Unwrap is
method (*StaleMapError).Unwrap is
#
# --- (c) mapping.NewIndex ---------------------------------------------------
# Production never calls it (Loader.Load builds the served index with
# buildIndex); it is a two-line wrapper over that same buildIndex, and its ~80
# callers are _test.go files in internal/audit, internal/match, internal/scout
# and internal/mapping. It stays because "move it to a test-support package" is
# not available: Index's only field is the unexported byAniList map, so NO
# package outside internal/mapping can construct one without an exported
# constructor in a non-test file (an export_test.go alias would serve only
# mapping's own test binary, not the three other packages). The alternatives
# are exporting the map - publishing mutable internal state to every consumer -
# or routing the call sites through the network-backed Loader, which would stop
# those tests pinning match/audit/scout behavior against a real index.
function NewIndex is

#
# (*sanitizedError).Unwrap — same errors-chain class as the wrappers above.
# sanitizedError (internal/mapping/mapping.go) exists so a Fribb-refresh failure
# can reach a log boundary as pre-sanitized, length-bounded text while the
# original cause stays reachable for classification: Error() returns the safe
# text, Unwrap() returns the cause. It is constructed in non-test code
# (mapping.go, wrapping the error through runesafe.SanitizeSingleLineBounded),
# and Unwrap is reached only through the stdlib's anonymous
# `interface{ Unwrap() error }`, which no reference search can see. Dropping it
# would leave a wrapper that hides its cause from every errors.Is/As caller
# while still replacing the message — the exact shape that makes a shutdown or a
# typed upstream failure unclassifiable.
method (*sanitizedError).Unwrap is
